// Companion to 1112: an input field and an output field that share the name
// `verdict` are DISTINCT fields (`input.verdict` / `output.verdict`), matched
// here by DISTINCT literals that BOTH hold in the event, in a PERMITTING
// policy — pinning that two same-named collided slots are never treated as a
// contradiction.
//
// This is the failure the current compiler exhibits: `field_slot_name` strips
// the `input.`/`output.` prefix, collapsing both into one `verdict` slot; RC4's
// duplicate-slot accumulation then emits `Eq("approve", "published")` — a
// contradiction — so the leaf is unsatisfiable and NEVER permits, even though
// the event genuinely carries input.verdict="approve" AND output.verdict=
// "published". The interpreter matches the two nested paths independently and
// permits. (Contrast 1112, which matches on a mismatch; here both constraints
// are simultaneously satisfiable and the correct verdict is PERMIT.)
//
// A Publish is permitted only if there was formerly a Review response whose
// requested verdict was "approve" AND whose recorded system verdict was
// "published".
//
// Controls:
//   trace_1: Review input.verdict="approve", output.verdict="published"
//            (both hold, distinct) -> Publish PERMITTED.
//   trace_2: Review input.verdict="approve", output.verdict="rejected"
//            (output.verdict differs) -> the output.verdict:"published"
//            constraint fails -> NOT permitted.
permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Publish",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 1h
        Drupe::Action::"Review"::response{ input.verdict: "approve", output.verdict: "published" }
};
